INT. HOTEL BALLROOM - EMPTY - NIGHT

Round tables, white cloths. A raised dais. A banner: "CONGRATULATIONS, BARRY & PETRA." Empty champagne flutes.

BARRY "BAZ" WOJCIECHOWSKI-OKAFOR, 40s, in a rumpled tux, stands behind the microphone. A tape recorder sits on the podium beside him.

He taps the mic. Feedback.

BAZ
Good evening, everyone. I'm Barry, the best man.

He pauses. Nobody is there.

BAZ (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Okay. Rehearsal.

He presses RECORD on the tape. Clears his throat.

BAZ (CONT'D)
Petra and I go way back. To fourth grade, when she punched me in the face.

He stops. Presses STOP. REWIND. PLAY.

BAZ (V.O.)
(tape)
...when she punched me in the face.

He winces.

BAZ
Too violent.

He presses RECORD again.

BAZ (CONT'D)
Petra and I go way back. To fourth grade, when she gave me a high-five.

PLAY.

BAZ (V.O.)
(tape)
...gave me a high-five.

BAZ
Too nice. She hit me.

He sighs. RECORD.

BAZ (CONT'D)
To the happy couple. May your marriage be like a good wine. Aged, complex, and occasionally bitter.

PLAY.

BAZ (V.O.)
(tape)
...occasionally bitter.

BAZ
Horrible. Terrible. Delete.

He slumps over the podium. Stares at the empty room. Slowly, he straightens.

BAZ (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Petra. Barry. I'm so happy for you both. I mean it.

PLAY.

BAZ (V.O.)
(tape, quiet)
I'm so happy for you both. I mean it.

He listens. Nods once. He tucks the tape recorder into his pocket, adjusts his tie, and walks off the dais, whistling.
